{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788193565,"nonce":"8tO6/O+WBkkegU6KlNQZ36mkRpw=","title":"Verify the issue backlog before building to it — filed diagnoses mix confirmed source with inference, and two were wrong","message":"Evan, 2026-08-31: \"we should have a run of agents verifying the issues\nthemselves, an issue about verifying issues.\"\n\nTHE PROBLEM, with evidence rather than worry\n\n14 issues were filed on 2026-08-31, most of them from PLAYING — driving the\nbench and noticing something break. That is a good source of issues and a bad\nsource of causes. A live observation tells you WHAT happened; the cause is an\ninference, and mine were stated in issue bodies with the same confidence as\nmember-cited source reads.\n\nTwo were wrong:\n\n- **`1eb2262`** — I wrote that spawning a pawn reordered the roster. It did not.\n  `pawns` was ALREADY deterministically sorted; the real cause is that the\n  attention score sums `100 - mood_pct`, so two colonists one mood point apart\n  trade places on any tick that moves either mood. **A fix built to the issue as\n  written would have left the actual bug in place.** The worker caught it only\n  because it investigated rather than implemented.\n- **`cc8988c`** — I argued \"the fight is over\" required judgement, by conflating\n  `map.dangerWatcher.DangerRating` with `threats.hostiles`. They disagreed by 3\n  on the live bench.\n\nBoth are corrected ON their issues now. The pattern is the problem, not those two.\n\nTHE RISK: a worker picks up an issue, builds exactly what the body says, and\nships a correct implementation of a cause that is not real. That costs a full\nround trip AND leaves the bug.\n\nTHE TASK\n\nA verification pass over the open backlog before any of it is dispatched. For\neach issue, an agent checks the body's factual claims and comments with a\nverdict — not a rewrite, a verdict:\n\n- **CONFIRMED** — the claim resolves to the cited FILE + MEMBER and says what\n  the issue says it says.\n- **CORRECTED** — the mechanism is different; state the real one with citations.\n- **UNVERIFIABLE FROM SOURCE** — a live-behaviour claim needing the bench. Name\n  what to run.\n- **INFERENCE, UNMARKED** — the body states as fact something that was actually\n  concluded from observation. Flag it even when it turns out to be right; the\n  next reader needs to know which kind of claim it is.\n\nPriority order: the 14 filed 2026-08-31 first (they are the newest and the least\nreviewed), then the older backlog. Anything labelled `state:next` before\nanything in `backlog`.\n\nRULES FOR THE VERIFYING AGENT\n\n- **Do not fix anything.** This is a verification pass. A tempting fix goes in\n  the comment as a recommendation.\n- Cite FILE + MEMBER, never bare line numbers — the two decompiled trees have\n  different offsets.\n- Separate what you CONFIRMED by reading from what you are inferring. That is\n  the whole point of the exercise; an unmarked inference in a verification\n  comment is worse than one in the original issue.\n- Where an issue's acceptance section is untestable as written, say so. Several\n  were written before the bench fixture requirements were understood, and 3.4's\n  fixture table was wrong by omission in four separate ways.\n\nWHY IT IS WORTH THE TOKENS: every issue a worker investigated rather than\nimplemented today came back better than its spec — `1a072fa` found the gate had\ntwo widgets and the issue cited one; `e8f2c32` found the load-bearing part was a\nside effect, not a gate; `32b9e01` found a third mutation the body missed. The\npattern is consistent enough to systematise.\n\nAcceptance\n- Every open issue carries a verification comment with one of the four verdicts.\n- Every CORRECTED issue's body-level error is stated plainly enough that a\n  worker reading only the issue cannot follow the stale version.\n- A summary naming which issues changed materially, so the muster handover can\n  point at them.","files":null}]}